[{:db/id 17592186092234 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-79a5f235-c3e4-525b-8b8a-0996cfbfc3bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092233 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-111ba920-c11a-5d82-a4be-8a7616b01f46" "question-301cb6c3-bb47-52a5-b9af-9305c0ba1695"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092235 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3beff671-5436-54ac-a3ba-4902dc435008"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092687 :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =5.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -10 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{-5}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186090322 :answer-field/correct 17592186090324 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186287675 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=6,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{6}{-2}=-3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186357565 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =8.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]-8[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 \\\\ 6 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-12{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-12,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-12}{-2}=6.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-79a5f235-c3e4-525b-8b8a-0996cfbfc3bb" :question/id #uuid "79a5f235-c3e4-525b-8b8a-0996cfbfc3bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107107" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=-1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-1}{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dd938821-c74e-5b26-90b9-683b5bbf18c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dd938821-c74e-5b26-90b9-683b5bbf18c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd938821-c74e-5b26-90b9-683b5bbf18c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3455a04a-3d31-5874-9183-71fedec9fd3c" "answer-708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" "answer-7d7d641b-bdc6-59a8-8ec2-2c684ab7d2ad" "answer-1b7b31d6-cbf3-5cfa-90f0-9eb9c7ff1c04" "answer-c26f161b-d55c-5ad7-8a54-c13c5aa07aba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3455a04a-3d31-5874-9183-71fedec9fd3c" :answer/id #uuid "3455a04a-3d31-5874-9183-71fedec9fd3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" :answer/id #uuid "708ac4ba-c186-57c1-8e54-0b82ac4b423d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d7d641b-bdc6-59a8-8ec2-2c684ab7d2ad" :answer/id #uuid "7d7d641b-bdc6-59a8-8ec2-2c684ab7d2ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b7b31d6-cbf3-5cfa-90f0-9eb9c7ff1c04" :answer/id #uuid "1b7b31d6-cbf3-5cfa-90f0-9eb9c7ff1c04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c26f161b-d55c-5ad7-8a54-c13c5aa07aba" :answer/id #uuid "c26f161b-d55c-5ad7-8a54-c13c5aa07aba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-111ba920-c11a-5d82-a4be-8a7616b01f46" :question/id #uuid "111ba920-c11a-5d82-a4be-8a7616b01f46" :question/math-academy-id "q-80273" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-1.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a06f774-759e-5d50-b56f-887b8f0a8b18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a06f774-759e-5d50-b56f-887b8f0a8b18" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a06f774-759e-5d50-b56f-887b8f0a8b18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9695b750-78da-5c59-9e1c-40fb8948f0d5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9695b750-78da-5c59-9e1c-40fb8948f0d5" "answer-3cbf235c-2ab7-5f04-a833-678137504436" "answer-1123d852-cba2-552b-8381-92943f50c468" "answer-270f4122-201c-5e17-b091-b38dd1de9097" "answer-22dd36d7-fc53-51a0-aab3-39d55a21db8d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9695b750-78da-5c59-9e1c-40fb8948f0d5" :answer/id #uuid "9695b750-78da-5c59-9e1c-40fb8948f0d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cbf235c-2ab7-5f04-a833-678137504436" :answer/id #uuid "3cbf235c-2ab7-5f04-a833-678137504436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1123d852-cba2-552b-8381-92943f50c468" :answer/id #uuid "1123d852-cba2-552b-8381-92943f50c468" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-270f4122-201c-5e17-b091-b38dd1de9097" :answer/id #uuid "270f4122-201c-5e17-b091-b38dd1de9097" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22dd36d7-fc53-51a0-aab3-39d55a21db8d" :answer/id #uuid "22dd36d7-fc53-51a0-aab3-39d55a21db8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186392010 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -17\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 \\\\ 9 & -15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}18 & -30 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n18{x}_{1}-30{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-301cb6c3-bb47-52a5-b9af-9305c0ba1695" :question/id #uuid "301cb6c3-bb47-52a5-b9af-9305c0ba1695" :question/math-academy-id "q-18081" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector  of $A=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -10{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ -10 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n10{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ae3ba2a-4875-59fc-9d7d-d6fd0283faf6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ae3ba2a-4875-59fc-9d7d-d6fd0283faf6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae3ba2a-4875-59fc-9d7d-d6fd0283faf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-51a64db1-d17d-5f2e-8521-b7c023059e26" "answer-e8e801b2-7526-5207-9e22-764415b22da1" "answer-a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" "answer-24127ac8-a8dc-5354-a501-9b79d9e9cc82" "answer-0684715f-1934-531e-a1eb-44980925a4b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51a64db1-d17d-5f2e-8521-b7c023059e26" :answer/id #uuid "51a64db1-d17d-5f2e-8521-b7c023059e26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8e801b2-7526-5207-9e22-764415b22da1" :answer/id #uuid "e8e801b2-7526-5207-9e22-764415b22da1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" :answer/id #uuid "a2e21edc-e126-5eb1-b060-bf1813c74e47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24127ac8-a8dc-5354-a501-9b79d9e9cc82" :answer/id #uuid "24127ac8-a8dc-5354-a501-9b79d9e9cc82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0684715f-1934-531e-a1eb-44980925a4b7" :answer/id #uuid "0684715f-1934-531e-a1eb-44980925a4b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244044 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}+18{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 18 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =29,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -6 \\\\ -12 & 25\\end{aligned}]-29[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-18{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ -12 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-18 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186244043 :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-3beff671-5436-54ac-a3ba-4902dc435008" :question/id #uuid "3beff671-5436-54ac-a3ba-4902dc435008" :question/math-academy-id "q-107306" :question/problem "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =17$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 15\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & 15 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =17,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 20 & 13\\end{aligned}]-17[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 \\\\ 20 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-15{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-15 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99f559f8-f0c3-5b01-8f1d-d740f6f545ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99f559f8-f0c3-5b01-8f1d-d740f6f545ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "99f559f8-f0c3-5b01-8f1d-d740f6f545ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa601251-b637-5aff-9c87-3c9e773948b0" "answer-6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" "answer-a651edc0-0c43-5e03-bb3e-0a76e26d7201" "answer-b67258fc-1571-58d7-b3b9-ff66e2487d06" "answer-f27efeb8-a34b-557e-9386-96c0b3190fdb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa601251-b637-5aff-9c87-3c9e773948b0" :answer/id #uuid "aa601251-b637-5aff-9c87-3c9e773948b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" :answer/id #uuid "6e2d8f93-5ef1-55d1-b815-c1515a040b2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a651edc0-0c43-5e03-bb3e-0a76e26d7201" :answer/id #uuid "a651edc0-0c43-5e03-bb3e-0a76e26d7201" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b67258fc-1571-58d7-b3b9-ff66e2487d06" :answer/id #uuid "b67258fc-1571-58d7-b3b9-ff66e2487d06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f27efeb8-a34b-557e-9386-96c0b3190fdb" :answer/id #uuid "f27efeb8-a34b-557e-9386-96c0b3190fdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}]
